<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Customs and commercial revenue</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Customs and commercial revenue refers to state income from duties, tolls and taxes on trade, as opposed to land taxes. Empires that controlled ports, crossings and trade routes could draw large revenues from passing commerce, funding armies and courts. Examples include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a>'s taxation of shipping, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>'s duties on the spice trade, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>'s tolls at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the maritime customs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p></div>
